        "edits": [
          "      // The replacer array sets the serialized key order: message, label, level.\n      const keyOrder = [\"message\", \"label\", \"level\"];\n      const payload = pretty\n        ? JSON.stringify(\n            { label: event.label, level: event.level, message },\n            keyOrder,\n            2\n          )\n        : JSON.stringify(\n            { label: event.label, level: event.level, message },\n            keyOrder\n          );"
        ],
        "finalMessage": "I made the edit, but I couldn't re-lint it because running ESLint needs approval in this session. Instead I checked the change against the rule and the original behaviour by hand.\n\nI fixed both issues (1 and 2) in `json.ts:173-184`. The two object literals passed to `JSON.stringify` now list their keys in sorted order: `label`, `level`, `message`.\n\nJust reordering the keys would have changed the log output. `JSON.stringify` writes keys in the order the object lists them, so every JSON log line would have started with `label` instead of `message`. To keep the output exactly the same, both calls now pass a replacer array, `[\"message\", \"label\", \"level\"]`, which controls the order keys are written in. The output stays the same:\n- A `null` label is still written as `\"label\":null`.\n- Pretty mode still indents by 2.\n- All three values are strings or `null`, so the array doesn't drop anything.\n\nThe array is a plain list of strings, not an object, so `sort-keys` doesn't check it. No suppression comments or config changes were added.",
